  "cli_result_text": "Submitted the file set needed to trace this error-provenance path across both repos:\n\n**Netty side (channel pipeline exception handling):**\n- `DefaultChannelPipeline.java` \u2014 tail-of-pipeline `exceptionCaught` fallback (logs and drops unhandled exceptions if no handler catches them)\n- `AbstractChannelHandlerContext.java` \u2014 `fireExceptionCaught` propagation mechanics through the handler chain\n- `ChannelInboundHandlerAdapter.java` / `ChannelInboundInvoker.java` / `ChannelHandlerContext.java` \u2014 the handler contract exceptions flow through\n- `ChannelInitializer.java` \u2014 pipeline construction, where handler ordering affects exception visibility\n- `HttpContentDecoder.java`, `HttpClientCodec.java` \u2014 concrete codec-level `fireExceptionCaught` call sites (representative of where connection-reset-style errors originate)\n\n**Spring WebFlux side (reactor-netty bridge and error handling):**\n- `ReactorHttpHandlerAdapter.java` \u2014 the actual bridge between reactor-netty's `HttpServerRequest`/`HttpServerResponse` and Spring's `HttpHandler`; this is where a Netty-originated exception first crosses into Spring's abstraction\n- `ReactorServerHttpRequest.java` \u2014 wraps the reactor-netty request\n- `HttpWebHandlerAdapter.java` \u2014 top-level `HttpHandler` that catches unresolved errors and maps them to a generic response (this is the likely \"context loss\" point \u2014 arbitrary exceptions get collapsed to a generic 500)\n- `WebHttpHandlerBuilder.java` \u2014 assembles the `WebExceptionHandler` chain and `ExceptionHandlingWebHandler` decorator\n- `ExceptionHandlingWebHandler.java` / `WebExceptionHandler.java` / `WebHandler.java` \u2014 the exception-handling chain itself\n- `ExceptionHandlingWebHandlerTests.java` \u2014 documents (in test form) what \"unresolved\" errors look like once they reach `HttpWebHandlerAdapter`\n- `ReactorHttpServer.java` (test fixture) \u2014 shows how the adapter is wired to an actual reactor-netty server for end-to-end tracing",
